<title>USA: EU grain subsidies too high, USDA official says.</title>
<headline>EU grain subsidies too high, USDA official says.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>USDA General Sales Manager Chris Goldthwait said Monday that the European Union's subsidy levels were too high and that the USDA does not accept their argument that subsidies are necessary.</p>
<p>&quot;Clearly their levels of subsidation are too high,&quot; Goldthwait said at a seminar on food security at USDA's annual Agricultural Outlook Forum.</p>
<p>Goldthwait said that the EU subsidies are taking money not only out of the pockets of U.S. wheat farmers, but also hurting the income of farmers in developing nations by lowering the price of world wheat.</p>
<p>Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman earlier in the day told the same conference that the U.S. subsidy program, the Export Enhancement Program, is his &quot;big stick&quot; and that Washington is prepared to use it to fight unfair competition.</p>
</text>
